Mastering Git & GitHub Commands for DevOps Interviews! 🚀

Whether you’re a beginner or an experienced DevOps Engineer, Git and GitHub form the backbone of version control and collaboration.

 In interviews, many practical and scenario-based Git questions are asked understanding the why and how behind each command makes all the difference.

Here are some of the most commonly used Git & GitHub commands every DevOps Engineer should know 👇

🔹 Repository Setup & Initialization
git init → Initialize a new Git repository
git clone <repo-url> → Clone an existing repository
git remote add origin <url> → Link local repo to remote GitHub repo

🔹 Staging & Committing Changes
git status → Check status of files
git add . → Stage all changes
git commit -m "message" → Commit changes with a message

🔹 Branching & Merging
git branch → List branches
git checkout -b <branch> → Create and switch to a new branch
git merge <branch> → Merge a branch into the current one

🔹 Working with Remote Repos
git push origin <branch> → Push changes to remote
git pull origin <branch> → Fetch + merge changes from remote
git fetch → Download latest commits without merging

🔹 Undo & Clean Up
git reset --hard HEAD~1 → Undo last commit
git revert <commit-id> → Create a new commit that undoes changes
git stash / git stash pop → Temporarily save and restore work

🔹 Advanced & Useful Options
git log --oneline --graph → Visualize commit history
git diff → Compare file differences
git tag v1.0 → Tag a release version

💡 Pro Tip: Always follow Git best practices → use clear commit messages, maintain branch naming conventions, and regularly rebase to keep a clean history.
